The PQXDH model in this repository is written in
[VCV-io](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/VCV-io/tree/88efe9715a9fa9e7d89635c38cf70259836766c2),
a Lean framework for cryptographic proofs. This chapter introduces the parts of
VCV-io that the later chapters use. VCV-io keeps three notions apart.

1. A computation is a tree of oracle queries. It records which queries it makes and
   how the computation continues after each answer. It does not say how a query is answered.
2. A query implementation answers the queries inside a monad. Running a computation
   with an implementation gives a computation in that monad.
3. A probability semantics turns a computation into a measure on its outputs. Failure
   is the mass that the measure does not have.

The first four sections follow this order. The fifth section shows how this repository
writes security experiments and advantages with these notions.

The cards in the first four sections show upstream code at the pinned commits of
VCV-io and [cslib](https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/tree/5b56df99362532c46e1175381fbbdd755bd56030).
The book does not track their status. Three cards in the fifth section come from this
repository. One of them is a theorem, and the book gives its status from the repository's
ledger of admitted lemmas.

# Oracle specifications

Let $`I` be a type. We define an _oracle specification_ with index type $`I` to be a
family of types $`R : I \to \mathrm{Type}`. We call an element $`i : I` a _query_ and
$`R(i)` the type of _answers_ to $`i`.

::::definition "oracle_spec" (lean := "OracleSpec")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleSpec -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The Lean definition is the family itself: `OracleSpec ι` is the type of functions
`ι → Type v`. For a specification `spec`, VCV-io writes `spec.Domain` for the index type
and `spec.Range i` for the answer type of the query `i`.
:::
::::

An oracle specification is the same thing as a polynomial functor, and this point of
view gives the computations of the next section. Recall that a
polynomial functor $`P` is given by a type $`A` of _positions_ and, for each
$`a : A`, a type $`B(a)` of _directions_. It acts on types by
$$`P(X) = \sum_{a : A} X^{B(a)}.`
We call $`P` the _polynomial_ of the specification $`R` if $`A = I` and $`B = R`. An
element of $`P(X)` is a query $`i` together with a map $`R(i) \to X`: the query, and what
happens for each possible answer.

::::definition "oracle_spec_to_pfunctor" (lean := "OracleSpec.toPFunctor") (uses := "oracle_spec")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleSpec.toPFunctor -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The Lean definition builds the polynomial functor with positions `ι` and directions
`spec`. The inverse construction is `OracleSpec.ofPFunctor`, and the lemmas
`toPFunctor_ofPFunctor` and `ofPFunctor_toPFunctor` show that the two constructions
are inverse to each other.
:::
::::

The basic example is `unifSpec`. Its index type is $`\mathbb{N}`, and the answer type of
the query $`n` is $`\{0, \dots, n\}`, written `Fin (n + 1)` in Lean.

# Computations as free monads

Let $`P` be a polynomial functor with positions $`A` and directions $`B`, and let
$`\alpha` be a type. We define $`\mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` to be the inductive type of
trees generated by two constructors:

* a leaf $`\mathrm{pure}(x)` for each $`x : \alpha`;
* a node $`\mathrm{liftBind}(a, k)` for each position $`a : A` and each map
  $`k : B(a) \to \mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)`.

Equivalently, $`\mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` is the least solution of
$`T \cong \alpha + P(T)`. We call $`\mathrm{Free}_P` the _free monad_ on $`P`. Its unit is
$`\mathrm{pure}`, and the bind of a tree $`t` with $`f : \alpha \to \mathrm{Free}_P(\beta)`
replaces every leaf $`\mathrm{pure}(x)` of $`t` with the tree $`f(x)`. A path from the root to
a leaf chooses one answer at every node on the way, so it is a transcript of the
interaction.

::::definition "free_monad" (lean := "PFunctor.FreeM")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair PFunctor.FreeM -docstrings
*Origin: cslib at the pin.*

The two constructors of the Lean inductive type are `pure` and `liftBind`, as above.
VCV-io reaches this type through [PolyFun](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/PolyFun/tree/ef4c157b3c6775fb55864303d96f9b400428d5f0),
which re-exports it.
:::
::::

Let $`R` be an oracle specification with polynomial $`P`. We call an element of
$`\mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` an _oracle computation_ for $`R` with outputs in $`\alpha`.

::::definition "oracle_comp" (lean := "OracleComp") (uses := "oracle_spec_to_pfunctor, free_monad")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleComp -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

`OracleComp spec` is by definition the free monad on `spec.toPFunctor`. A single query,
an element of $`P(X)`, has its own type `OracleQuery spec`.
:::
::::

We call an oracle computation for `unifSpec` a _probabilistic computation_. The query
$`n` asks for an element of $`\{0, \dots, n\}`. Section 4 interprets the answer as a uniform
sample.

::::definition "prob_comp" (lean := "ProbComp") (uses := "oracle_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbComp -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

`ProbComp` is an abbreviation for `OracleComp unifSpec`. Lean writes a uniform sample
from a finite type `T` as `$ᵗ T`. It is a `ProbComp` computation.
:::
::::

# Implementing oracles

Let $`R` be an oracle specification and let $`m` be a monad. We define a _query
implementation_ of $`R` in $`m` to be a family $`\theta = (\theta_i)_{i : I}` with
$`\theta_i \in m(R(i))`. Each query thus gets a computation in $`m` that produces its answer.

::::definition "query_impl" (lean := "QueryImpl") (uses := "oracle_spec")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair QueryImpl -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The Lean definition is the dependent function type `(x : spec.Domain) → m (spec.Range x)`.
:::
::::

Let $`\theta` be a query implementation of $`R` in $`m`. We define
$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta : \mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha) \to m(\alpha)` by recursion on trees:
$$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta(\mathrm{pure}(x)) = \mathrm{pure}(x), \qquad
\mathrm{sim}_\theta(\mathrm{liftBind}(i, k)) = \theta_i \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} (b \mapsto \mathrm{sim}_\theta(k(b))).`
So $`\mathrm{sim}_\theta` replaces each query node with the computation that the
implementation gives for it.

::::definition "simulate_q" (lean := "simulateQ") (uses := "query_impl, oracle_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair simulateQ -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

`simulateQ impl` is cslib's fold `PFunctor.FreeM.liftM impl`, whose two defining
equations are the two equations above.
:::
::::

::::theorem "simulate_q_bind" (lean := "simulateQ_bind") (uses := "simulate_q")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair simulateQ_bind (part := "signature") -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

Let $`m` be a lawful monad. Then
$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta(t \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f) = \mathrm{sim}_\theta(t) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} (\mathrm{sim}_\theta \circ f)`.
:::
::::

Together with `simulateQ_pure`, which holds by definition, this theorem says that
$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta` is a monad morphism. PolyFun proves that it is the only one that
agrees with $`\theta` on single queries (`PFunctor.FreeM.liftMHom_unique`). This is the
universal property of the free monad: query implementations in a lawful monad $`m` are
the same as monad morphisms from $`\mathrm{Free}_P` to $`m`.

Security games use this to give an adversary its oracles. For example, the UAKE game
of the next chapter answers the adversary's queries in a state monad that holds the
sessions, and `simulateQ` turns the adversary into a computation in that monad.

# Output distributions and failure

A probability semantics needs a distribution for the answers to each query. Let $`P` be a
polynomial functor whose direction types $`B(a)` are measurable spaces. We define an
_answer measure_ for $`P` to be a family $`\nu = (\nu_a)_{a : A}` of probability measures, with
$`\nu_a` a measure on $`B(a)`. In Lean this is the class `PFunctor.AnswerMeasure`. For an
oracle specification, VCV-io gives every answer type the discrete σ-algebra. For
`unifSpec`, the chosen answer measure is uniform on $`\{0, \dots, n\}`.

Let $`\nu` be an answer measure for $`P` and let $`\alpha` be a measurable space. We define
the _denotation_ $`[\![\cdot]\!] : \mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha) \to \mathrm{Measure}(\alpha)` by recursion on trees:
$$`[\![\mathrm{pure}(x)]\!] = \delta_x, \qquad
[\![\mathrm{liftBind}(a, k)]\!] = \int [\![k(b)]\!] \, d\nu_a(b),`
where the integral is the bind of the Giry monad. The second equation applies when
$`b \mapsto [\![k(b)]\!]` is $`\nu_a`-almost everywhere measurable. Otherwise the denotation
of the node is the zero measure.

::::definition "free_denote" (lean := "PFunctor.FreeM.denote") (uses := "free_monad")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair PFunctor.FreeM.denote -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The Lean definition has the two cases above. The measurability test is an `if` with
`else 0`.
:::
::::

VCV-io gives a measure semantics to other monads as well. We say that a monad $`m` has a
_measure semantics_ if every $`x \in m(\alpha)` comes with a measure on $`\alpha` of total mass at
most $`1`. In Lean this is the class `EvalDistSemantics`.

::::definition "eval_dist" (lean := "evalDist") (uses := "free_denote")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair evalDist -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

`evalDist mx` is the measure that the semantics of the monad gives `mx`. VCV-io writes it
`𝒟[mx]`. For an oracle computation, `𝒟[mx]` is the denotation above, and the lemma
`PFunctor.FreeM.evalDist_eq_denote` says so.
:::
::::

Let $`x \in m(\alpha)` and let $`\mu` be its measure. We define the _failure probability_ of
$`x` to be $`1 - \mu(\alpha)`. VCV-io denotes the probability of an event by
`Pr{…}[…]`. The expression `Pr{let _ ← mx}[True]` is the total mass $`\mu(\alpha)`.

::::definition "pr_fail" (lean := "prFail") (uses := "eval_dist")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair prFail -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The Lean definition maps every output to `True` and subtracts the probability of `True`
from $`1`. The lemma `prFail_def` restates this as `prFail mx = 1 - Pr{let _ ← mx}[True]`.
:::
::::

For an oracle computation, the only place where the denotation loses mass is the
`else 0` case. Answer types of an oracle specification carry the discrete σ-algebra, so
that case never occurs, and no mass is lost.

::::theorem "oracle_comp_lossless" (lean := "OracleComp.prFail_eq_zero") (uses := "pr_fail, oracle_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleComp.prFail_eq_zero (part := "signature") -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

Let $`R` be an oracle specification with an answer measure. Every oracle computation for
$`R` has failure probability $`0`.
:::
::::

Failure therefore comes from the monad in which an experiment runs. For example, the
semantics of `OptionT m` pulls the measure of the underlying computation back along
`some`, so a result `none` is lost mass.

# Experiments and advantages

The experiments in this repository are written over an arbitrary monad $`m`. Let $`m` be a
monad. We define a _runtime_ for $`m` to be a measure semantics for $`m` together with a
monad morphism $`\mathrm{ProbComp} \to m`, which samples public randomness inside $`m`. The
semantics must commute with measurable maps.

::::definition "prob_comp_runtime" (lean := "ProbCompRuntime") (uses := "eval_dist, prob_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbCompRuntime -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The three fields are the semantics, the sampling morphism, and the law for measurable maps.
The canonical runtime `ProbCompRuntime.probComp` takes $`m = \mathrm{ProbComp}`, the
identity morphism, and the semantics `𝒟`. The lemma `ProbCompRuntime.probComp_evalDist`
says that it evaluates computations by `𝒟`. By the previous section, no computation
loses mass under this runtime. The PQXDH security theorems of this repository are stated
for this runtime.
:::
::::

We call a computation with output in $`\mathrm{Bool}` a _security experiment_. Its outcome is
read through the measure $`\mu` that the runtime gives it. VCV-io measures the advantage of
an adversary by the bias of $`\mu`.

Let $`\mu` be a measure on $`\mathrm{Bool}`. We define the _bias_ of $`\mu` to be
$$`\mathrm{bias}(\mu) = |\mu\{\mathrm{true}\} - \mu\{\mathrm{false}\}| \in [0, \infty].`

::::definition "bool_bias" (lean := "MeasureTheory.Measure.boolBias")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair MeasureTheory.Measure.boolBias -docstrings
*Origin: VCV-io at the pin.*

The Lean definition takes the absolute difference of the two masses in the extended
non-negative reals. For example, VCV-io's `KEMScheme.IND_CCA_Advantage` is the bias of the IND-CCA game.
That game outputs whether the adversary guessed the challenge bit.
:::
::::

This repository uses two other conventions for the UAKE game of the next chapter. Let
$`\mathsf{G}` be that experiment, and let $`\mu_{\mathsf{G}}` be its measure under a runtime.
We define the _advantage_ of an adversary $`\mathcal{A}` to be
$$`\mathrm{Adv}(\mathcal{A}) = |\mu_{\mathsf{G}}\{\mathrm{true}\} - \tfrac{1}{2}|.`

::::definition "uake_advantage" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.advantage") (uses := "prob_comp_runtime")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.advantage -docstrings
*Origin: this repository.*

The value is a real number. The experiment `Exp` draws the challenge bit itself and
returns whether the adversary won.
:::
::::

For the second convention, let $`\mathsf{G}_0` and $`\mathsf{G}_1` be the experiment with the
challenge bit fixed to the real and the random case, respectively. Both return the
adversary's guess. We define the _distinguishing advantage_ to be
$$`\mathrm{DistAdv}(\mathcal{A}) = |\mu_{\mathsf{G}_1}\{\mathrm{true}\} - \mu_{\mathsf{G}_0}\{\mathrm{true}\}|.`

::::definition "uake_dist_advantage" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.distAdvantage") (uses := "prob_comp_runtime")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.distAdvantage -docstrings
*Origin: this repository.*

`FixedBitExp lift A false` is the real game and `FixedBitExp lift A true` the random game.
The value is VCV-io's `Measure.boolDist` of their two measures, converted to a real
number with `toReal`.
:::
::::

::::theorem "uake_advantage_eq_dist_advantage_div_two" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.advantage_eq_distAdvantage_div_two") (uses := "uake_advantage, uake_dist_advantage")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.advantage_eq_distAdvantage_div_two (part := "signature") -docstrings
*Origin: this repository. Status: proved.*

The ledger lists no admitted lemma in its proof, and its axioms are `propext`,
`Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.

Under the runtime `ProbCompRuntime.probComp`,
$`\mathrm{Adv}(\mathcal{A}) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{DistAdv}(\mathcal{A})`.
:::
::::

The three conventions differ by constant factors, and the factors hold only when the
experiment loses no mass. If $`\mu` is a probability measure on $`\mathrm{Bool}`, then
$`\mu\{\mathrm{true}\} + \mu\{\mathrm{false}\} = 1`, and so
$`\mathrm{bias}(\mu) = 2\,|\mu\{\mathrm{true}\} - \tfrac{1}{2}|`. The bias of a game that
outputs whether the adversary won is therefore twice the guess-form advantage. Two open
issues track the consequences.

* [Issue #29](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/libsignal-verify/issues/29):
  under a runtime that can lose mass, $`|\mu_{\mathsf{G}}\{\mathrm{true}\} - \tfrac{1}{2}|`
  counts lost mass as a loss for the adversary. If half of the mass is lost and the
  adversary wins on the other half, then $`\mu_{\mathsf{G}}\{\mathrm{true}\} = \tfrac{1}{2}`
  and the advantage is $`0`. An experiment that always fails has advantage $`\tfrac{1}{2}`.
  This does not affect the PQXDH theorems, which use `ProbCompRuntime.probComp`.
* [Issue #30](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/libsignal-verify/issues/30):
  the guess-form advantages of this repository are half of VCV-io's bias. A bound proved
  in one convention and used in the other is off by a factor of two.

The types differ as well. The bias takes values in $`[0, \infty]`, the extended
non-negative reals. The repository's advantages are real numbers. Where a theorem combines the
two, as the PQXDH theorems do with the IND-CCA advantage of the KEM, it converts the
bias to a real number with `toReal`.
